1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:03,660
Léa: Hello everyone and welcome to The Cozy French!

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:04,920
I'm Léa.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,440
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

4
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:08,280
Thank you for being with us.

5
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,820
Today, you can settle in

6
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:12,200
without preparing anything.

7
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,560
Léa: No list to finish before we start.

8
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:18,600
Just a moment to listen to French together.

9
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,920
Mathieu: Still, I brought an important document:

10
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,480
my armchair's loyalty card.

11
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,980
Léa: It hands out points now?

12
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:31,320
Mathieu: One box for every real break.

13
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:34,720
For now, my card is completely empty.

14
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:38,280
Léa: So your armchair is waiting for its first regular customer.

15
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:40,540
Today, we're talking about

16
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:42,920
slowing down without feeling guilty.

17
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:45,460
Taking your time can seem simple,

18
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:47,680
until the moment you try.

19
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:51,440
Mathieu: You sit down, then a little voice asks:

20
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,060
"Are you sure you don't have

21
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:55,660
anything more useful to do?"

22
00:00:55,710 --> 00:00:59,520
Léa: This conversation is for B1 and B2 levels.

23
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:01,270
We'll speak slowly

24
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:04,360
and explain everyday expressions.

25
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:05,010
Mathieu: By the end,

26
00:01:05,010 --> 00:01:08,080
you'll be able to talk about your need for rest

27
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:11,240
and ask whether a task can wait.

28
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:14,680
Léa: You'll also learn to set a limit simply,

29
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:17,560
without preparing a ten-minute speech.

30
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,730
Mathieu: You can listen on the train,

31
00:01:19,730 --> 00:01:22,160
at home or during a walk.

32
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,820
No need to understand every word.

33
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:26,810
Léa: Follow the stories and repeat

34
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:29,240
the phrases that will be useful to you.

35
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:32,480
Mathieu: And if you've already started making a list,

36
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,240
keep a little room for your armchair.

37
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,280
Léa: Here's our question of the day:

38
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:40,850
what would you like to do more slowly,

39
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:42,990
without feeling guilty?

40
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,150
Mathieu: Walking to the bakery?

41
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,070
Reading a few pages?

42
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,910
Finishing a conversation without looking at the time?

43
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:52,830
Léa: Keep one idea in mind.

44
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:53,450
At the end,

45
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:57,120
we'll give you a sentence to tell us about it.

46
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:00,030
Mathieu: And tell us where you're listening from.

47
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:02,560
A little break can bring us together,

48
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:06,280
even thousands of kilometres apart.

49
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:08,150
Léa: Last Sunday, I went

50
00:02:08,150 --> 00:02:10,120
to the media library in my neighbourhood.

51
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:13,400
I felt like reading a novel somewhere quiet.

52
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:15,030
Mathieu: Had you picked a book?

53
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,250
Léa: Yes, a story that takes place

54
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:18,600
by the sea.

55
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,900
I found an armchair near a big window.

56
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:25,720
Mathieu: Already, your armchair is better placed than mine.

57
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:27,000
It has a view.

58
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,600
Léa: It was raining gently.

59
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:31,320
I put down my coat and opened the book.

60
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:33,840
Then I thought about my week.

61
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,430
Mathieu: The sea disappeared?

62
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:39,320
Léa: Completely. In its place, I saw my appointments.

63
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,350
I started feeling guilty

64
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:43,280
because I wasn't preparing anything.

65
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:46,880
Mathieu: "Culpabiliser" means feeling guilty,

66
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:49,420
thinking you're doing something wrong?

67
00:02:49,470 --> 00:02:52,440
Léa: Yes, even when nobody blames us for anything.

68
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,880
For example, I sometimes feel guilty

69
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:56,840
about turning down an invitation.

70
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,850
Mathieu: And your novel was waiting while

71
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:01,110
you were blaming yourself.

72
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:03,680
Léa: Exactly. I took out my notebook.

73
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,740
I wrote down Monday's shopping,

74
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:08,960
a file to send and a present to buy.

75
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:11,710
Mathieu: So you'd borrowed a desk, in the end.

76
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,840
Léa: A very comfortable desk.

77
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:15,720
But after twenty minutes,

78
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:18,580
I was putting even more pressure on myself.

79
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:20,320
Mathieu: "Se mettre la pression"

80
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:25,280
means demanding a lot of yourself and creating stress.

81
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:27,450
Léa: Yes. Before a presentation,

82
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:29,190
my colleague puts pressure on himself

83
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:32,440
to answer every question perfectly.

84
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:34,760
Mathieu: He could start by answering the first one.

85
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,840
The rest will come later.

86
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:39,650
Léa: That day, I wanted to start Monday

87
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:41,240
with no surprises at all.

88
00:03:41,240 --> 00:03:44,820
So every line in my notebook led to another.

89
00:03:44,870 --> 00:03:47,530
Mathieu: You'd found a meeting where you were

90
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:51,160
both the organiser and the whole team.

91
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:54,440
Léa: With lots of requests and no biscuits.

92
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:57,150
Then I heard a little girl ask

93
00:03:57,150 --> 00:03:59,280
her father to look at the rain.

94
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,230
Mathieu: And you looked too?

95
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:03,400
Léa: Yes. Just a few seconds.

96
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:05,760
It reminded me why I'd come:

97
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:07,570
to switch off a little.

98
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:11,420
Mathieu: "Décrocher", here, means to stop thinking

99
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:15,640
about your worries and step back.

100
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:16,150
Léa: That's it.

101
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:18,760
For example, drawing helps me switch off

102
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:20,720
after a busy day.

103
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:23,270
Mathieu: So, did you manage to read?

104
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:26,360
Léa: A few pages. I put my notebook away,

105
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:29,760
even though my week wasn't fully organised.

106
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,030
Mathieu: And the sea came back.

107
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,140
Léa: Yes. With a character who had missed his boat.

108
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:39,440
I was delighted that appointment wasn't mine.

109
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,980
Mathieu: On Saturday, I took part in a repair workshop

110
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:44,800
at the community centre.

111
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:48,520
We help people fix small objects.

112
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:49,870
Léa: Do you go often?

113
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,600
Mathieu: Once a month.

114
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:54,320
This time, I was supposed to stay until noon.

115
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:57,920
After that, I'd planned a walk with a friend.

116
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:59,790
Léa: Were you able to leave on time?

117
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:03,100
Mathieu: No. I started overdoing it.

118
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:06,200
One person needed help with a zip,

119
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:08,560
another with a lamp.

120
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:10,960
Léa: "En faire trop" means going beyond

121
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:14,040
what you can reasonably do.

122
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:16,580
Mathieu: Like when I plan six activities

123
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:18,880
for a single day of holiday.

124
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:22,120
I overdo it and I rush around everywhere.

125
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:24,790
Léa: You weren't the only volunteer, though.

126
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:27,780
Mathieu: No, but I said yes before

127
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,240
looking at what was already waiting for me.

128
00:05:30,290 --> 00:05:33,120
By noon, I was completely snowed under.

129
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:37,240
Léa: You felt overwhelmed, with too many things to handle.

130
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:39,680
It's a figurative expression.

131
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:43,440
Mathieu: For example, this week, with the shop's orders,

132
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:46,280
my cousin is snowed under.

133
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,510
Léa: And your friend?

134
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:48,800
Mathieu: He called me.

135
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:51,520
I said: "Ten more minutes."

136
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:55,520
Except I had no idea how long it would take.

137
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:58,420
Léa: Ten very optimistic minutes.

138
00:05:58,470 --> 00:05:58,760
Mathieu: Yes.

139
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:00,900
Another volunteer asked me

140
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:03,120
what time I finished.

141
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:07,200
I answered: "Normally, half an hour ago."

142
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:08,960
Léa: She must have understood.

143
00:06:08,960 --> 00:06:11,840
Mathieu: She offered to take over.

144
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:13,720
She also advised me to

145
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:16,360
set my boundaries as soon as I arrive.

146
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:18,900
Léa: That's saying clearly what you accept

147
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:21,040
and how far you can go.

148
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:23,800
Mathieu: For example, for work calls,

149
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:25,460
I set my limits:

150
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:28,960
I'm available until six p.m.

151
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:31,180
Léa: So you can be useful without

152
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:33,840
staying until the last person leaves.

153
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:34,300
Mathieu: Yes.

154
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:38,480
Next time, I'll say my schedule at the start.

155
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:40,270
My friend was patient,

156
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:43,640
but our walk deserved a place too.

157
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:46,030
Léa: Did you apologise to him?

158
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:49,520
Mathieu: Yes. Then we walked at a relaxed pace.

159
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:52,460
I didn't offer to repair a single bench.

160
00:06:52,510 --> 00:06:53,880
Léa: In both our stories,

161
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:56,520
nobody was asking us to do everything.

162
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:00,280
And yet, we felt we had to keep going.

163
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:03,220
Mathieu: For me, leaving seemed to mean

164
00:07:03,220 --> 00:07:05,640
that I wasn't helpful enough.

165
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:08,440
Even with other volunteers there.

166
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:10,760
Léa: Me, I felt that an hour

167
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:13,400
without preparing was an hour wasted.

168
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:15,610
Mathieu: We sometimes confuse a wish

169
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:18,340
to do well with an emergency.

170
00:07:18,390 --> 00:07:20,820
Léa: An emergency is a situation

171
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:23,120
that calls for quick action.

172
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:26,320
But not every request is urgent.

173
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:30,760
Mathieu: For example, a big water leak is an emergency.

174
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:32,990
Choosing the colour of a poster

175
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:35,200
can usually wait.

176
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,080
Léa: And when the deadline isn't clear,

177
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:40,550
you can ask: "When do you need it by?"

178
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:43,620
Mathieu: That stops you inventing a deadline in your head.

179
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:45,880
I'm very good at giving myself

180
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:49,080
missions before the project has even started.

181
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:51,560
Léa: An obligation is different:

182
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:53,620
it's something you have to do,

183
00:07:53,620 --> 00:07:56,440
because of a commitment or a rule.

184
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:59,010
Mathieu: For example, keeping to this schedule

185
00:07:59,010 --> 00:08:02,200
is part of my obligations at work.

186
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,840
Léa: Some obligations are real.

187
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,680
You can't simply leave

188
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:09,640
your post or stop looking after a child.

189
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:13,100
Mathieu: A friend works at the front desk of a sports centre.

190
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:14,310
She can't close

191
00:08:14,310 --> 00:08:17,000
the counter whenever she wants to go for a walk.

192
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,160
Léa: So telling her to slow down isn't enough.

193
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:22,700
You also have to look at the organisation

194
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:24,800
and who can take over.

195
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:27,520
Mathieu: Yes. And having a free evening

196
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:31,240
doesn't always mean you still have energy.

197
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:34,680
Léa: Sometimes, you're at the end of your rope: you feel exhausted,

198
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:37,480
with very little strength left.

199
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:39,760
Mathieu: After several days of moving house,

200
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:41,850
my brother was exhausted.

201
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:44,040
He asked for help to finish.

202
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,580
Léa: Asking for help can matter more than a

203
00:08:46,580 --> 00:08:50,240
new method to fill your diary better.

204
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,720
Let's do a role play.

205
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:55,400
I'm your neighbour and I'm asking you a favour.

206
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:59,640
Mathieu: Listen to her request, then try to answer before me.

207
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:01,720
Léa: I'll be away this week.

208
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:05,670
Could you take in my parcels every day?

209
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:07,270
Mathieu: Your turn.

210
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:08,440
You want to help,

211
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:11,300
but you're only free on Tuesday.

212
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:15,200
What would you say? Take a few seconds.

213
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:18,380
Léa: You can start with: "I can…

214
00:09:18,430 --> 00:09:21,190
but I can't…"

215
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:23,690
Mathieu: I can take in your parcels on Tuesday,

216
00:09:23,690 --> 00:09:26,840
but I can't stay available all week.

217
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:28,050
Léa: Ah, OK.

218
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:31,070
You work from home the other days, though?

219
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:33,290
Mathieu: Yes, but I have appointments and I

220
00:09:33,290 --> 00:09:36,440
can't stop for every delivery.

221
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:37,720
Léa: I understand.

222
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:41,260
For Tuesday, shall I send you the details now?

223
00:09:41,310 --> 00:09:43,590
Mathieu: Can we put that off until later?

224
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:45,480
I'm leaving in a few minutes.

225
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:48,000
I'll be able to look this evening.

226
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,400
Léa: "Remettre à plus tard"

227
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:52,220
means deciding to do something afterwards,

228
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:54,480
instead of doing it now.

229
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:57,070
Mathieu: For example, we put the visit off

230
00:09:57,070 --> 00:10:00,200
until later because the museum was closed.

231
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:03,160
Léa: Very good. I'll send you a message this evening.

232
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:04,700
And I'll look for another

233
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:06,900
solution for the other days.

234
00:10:06,950 --> 00:10:11,060
Mathieu: Thanks. On Tuesday, I can receive the parcels

235
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:12,720
between nine and noon.

236
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:16,280
Léa: Perfect, I'll check if that time slot works.

237
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:19,370
Mathieu: There. I offered something specific,

238
00:10:19,370 --> 00:10:21,880
without promising my whole week.

239
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:23,230
Léa: And you didn't recount

240
00:10:23,230 --> 00:10:26,600
every appointment to justify your answer.

241
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:29,140
Mathieu: I could have. With the times,

242
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:32,360
the addresses and a little map of the neighbourhood.

243
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,220
Léa: A clear answer was enough.

244
00:10:35,270 --> 00:10:39,230
You can also say: "This time, I won't be able to."

245
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:42,950
Mathieu: Or: "I can help you for half an hour."

246
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,400
Even a short answer can be warm.

247
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:49,300
Léa: To start, you can choose a moment

248
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:52,320
when you really have some room to breathe.

249
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:55,800
Mathieu: Me, I'm going to ease off after the Saturday workshop.

250
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:59,600
I'll slow down instead of adding yet another activity.

251
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:02,140
Léa: That's what "lever le pied" means.

252
00:11:02,190 --> 00:11:05,080
Example: after this big project,

253
00:11:05,080 --> 00:11:08,840
our team is going to ease off for a few days.

254
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:10,850
Mathieu: So I'm keeping my walk,

255
00:11:10,850 --> 00:11:13,060
but I'm not turning

256
00:11:13,060 --> 00:11:15,800
the way home into an endless shopping list.

257
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:16,640
Léa: As for me,

258
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:18,340
I'm going to leave my notebook in

259
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,480
my bag when I get to the media library.

260
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:24,760
Mathieu: An idea can still come up while you're reading.

261
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:25,170
Léa: Yes.

262
00:11:25,170 --> 00:11:28,660
I can jot down a word, then go back to the book.

263
00:11:28,710 --> 00:11:30,540
I don't need to develop

264
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:32,640
the whole project right away.

265
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:34,000
Mathieu: And to take a breather,

266
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,270
do you necessarily need a long moment of calm?

267
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:41,520
Léa: No. "Souffler", here, means taking a little break.

268
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:43,520
After setting up the tables,

269
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:46,560
we took a breather for a few minutes in the courtyard.

270
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:48,790
Mathieu: Me, I could simply sit down

271
00:11:48,790 --> 00:11:50,960
without looking for something to listen to,

272
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:53,680
to tidy or to learn.

273
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:56,620
Léa: You can also walk or chat.

274
00:11:56,670 --> 00:11:57,220
There isn't

275
00:11:57,220 --> 00:12:00,160
one single right way to spend that moment.

276
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:02,630
Mathieu: And what if I still think about work?

277
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:03,880
Léa: That can happen.

278
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:05,380
You haven't failed your break

279
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:07,760
because a thought came back.

280
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:09,280
Mathieu: That reassures me.

281
00:12:09,280 --> 00:12:11,540
Otherwise, I was going to make a chart

282
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:14,040
to measure the quality of my breaks.

283
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:17,160
Léa: Instead, you can try to pace yourself:

284
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:19,790
use your energy carefully

285
00:12:19,790 --> 00:12:22,660
so you don't spend it all at once.

286
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:25,570
Mathieu: For example, on a long hike,

287
00:12:25,570 --> 00:12:26,960
I pace myself

288
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:30,240
by walking calmly from the start.

289
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:33,480
Léa: For me, it means keeping dinner simple

290
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:37,000
on evenings when I already have lots to do.

291
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:38,890
Mathieu: And if I tidy the whole

292
00:12:38,890 --> 00:12:41,200
kitchen to avoid a difficult message,

293
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:44,280
maybe that's not the break I wanted.

294
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:46,890
Léa: You can ask yourself what's holding you back,

295
00:12:46,890 --> 00:12:49,260
then choose when to reply.

296
00:12:49,310 --> 00:12:52,840
You don't need to sort it out right away.

297
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:55,200
Mathieu: Everyone can move forward at their own pace:

298
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:57,480
at a speed that suits them.

299
00:12:57,480 --> 00:12:57,870
Léa: Yes.

300
00:12:57,870 --> 00:13:01,760
In my drawing class, everyone learns at their own pace.

301
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:04,600
Nobody finishes at the same time.

302
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:06,770
Mathieu: My armchair will be happy.

303
00:13:06,770 --> 00:13:09,480
It's never liked competitions.

304
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:12,520
Léa: Now, go back to your idea from the start.

305
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:14,610
Complete this sentence:

306
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:18,780
"I'd like to take my time to…

307
00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:19,520
because…"

308
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:21,360
Mathieu: I'd like to take my time

309
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:23,360
walking after lunch,

310
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:27,640
because I like seeing what changes in my neighbourhood.

311
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:31,270
Léa: I'd like to take my time reading on Sundays,

312
00:13:31,270 --> 00:13:33,360
because I want to follow a story

313
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:36,560
without planning the rest of my day.

314
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:37,830
Mathieu: Your turn.

315
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:40,240
Say your sentence out loud.

316
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:43,940
You can use very simple words.

317
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:47,160
Léa: Then, add a possible limit:

318
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:50,720
"To protect this moment, I can…"

319
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,520
Mathieu: To protect this moment,

320
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:54,500
I can announce my leaving time

321
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:57,840
before the workshop starts.

322
00:13:57,840 --> 00:13:59,530
Léa: To protect this moment,

323
00:13:59,530 --> 00:14:02,060
I can make my shopping list

324
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:04,480
at another time of day.

325
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:08,040
Mathieu: Choose something that depends a little on you.

326
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:12,060
You don't need to reorganise your whole life.

327
00:14:12,110 --> 00:14:14,320
Léa: And if what you mostly need is help,

328
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:15,960
you can say:

329
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:18,910
"Could you take over?"

330
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:23,600
Mathieu: That's also a precious sentence to know how to say.

331
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:25,880
Léa: Let's go back over five expressions.

332
00:14:25,880 --> 00:14:29,800
Listen to their meaning, then repeat with us.

333
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:33,510
Mathieu: The first one is "se mettre la pression".

334
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:35,060
At the media library,

335
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:39,800
Léa wanted to plan a week with no surprises at all.

336
00:14:39,850 --> 00:14:42,720
Léa: It's setting yourself high standards,

337
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:45,670
sometimes hard to bear.

338
00:14:45,720 --> 00:14:49,000
New example: for this meal with friends,

339
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,800
I'm putting pressure on myself for nothing.

340
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:55,350
Mathieu: Let's repeat: se mettre la pression.

341
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:57,030
A dinner can start without

342
00:14:57,030 --> 00:15:00,960
the napkins being perfectly folded.

343
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:04,510
Léa: Second expression: avoir la tête sous l'eau.

344
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:09,100
At the workshop, you'd accepted too many little requests.

345
00:15:09,150 --> 00:15:12,000
Mathieu: Yes, I was overwhelmed.

346
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,550
Example: since the restaurant opened,

347
00:15:15,550 --> 00:15:18,680
the owners have been snowed under.

348
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:21,990
Léa: Let's repeat: avoir la tête sous l'eau.

349
00:15:22,040 --> 00:15:24,800
We're talking about all the tasks to handle,

350
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:26,480
not a swimming pool.

351
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:30,590
Mathieu: Third expression: poser ses limites.

352
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:33,350
I can announce my leaving time

353
00:15:33,350 --> 00:15:35,640
and what I can accept.

354
00:15:35,690 --> 00:15:39,760
Léa: It's stating what you can do and what you refuse.

355
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:42,190
Example: in this group,

356
00:15:42,190 --> 00:15:45,800
I set my limits on late calls.

357
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:49,110
Mathieu: Let's repeat: poser ses limites.

358
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:52,050
You can start with one sentence,

359
00:15:52,050 --> 00:15:54,680
without organising a conference.

360
00:15:54,680 --> 00:15:57,950
Léa: Fourth expression: lever le pied.

361
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,010
You want to slow down after your Saturday activity.

362
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:05,840
Mathieu: It means reducing your pace or your activity.

363
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:08,380
Example: during the holidays,

364
00:16:08,380 --> 00:16:12,840
she eases off and keeps her mornings free.

365
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:15,510
Léa: Let's repeat: lever le pied.

366
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:17,900
You can say it about work,

367
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:21,320
activities or a busy schedule.

368
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:24,920
Mathieu: Finally, ménager ses forces.

369
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:27,180
During a long day,

370
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:30,140
you try to save your energy.

371
00:16:30,190 --> 00:16:32,980
Léa: Example: before the evening show,

372
00:16:32,980 --> 00:16:35,100
the dancers save their strength

373
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:37,240
during their free time.

374
00:16:37,240 --> 00:16:40,670
Mathieu: Let's repeat: ménager ses forces.

375
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:43,260
It doesn't necessarily mean stopping everything,

376
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:47,040
but choosing where you put your energy.

377
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,560
Léa: Now choose one expression.

378
00:16:49,560 --> 00:16:52,100
Think of a situation that's your own

379
00:16:52,100 --> 00:16:55,760
and build your own sentence.

380
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:59,340
Mathieu: Even if you're searching for words, take your time.

381
00:16:59,390 --> 00:17:03,360
Here too, you can go at your own pace.

382
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,980
Léa: In the comments, write:

383
00:17:06,030 --> 00:17:08,810
"I'd like to take my time to…

384
00:17:08,860 --> 00:17:13,150
because…" We'll be happy to read your ideas.

385
00:17:13,200 --> 00:17:16,260
Mathieu: And if you've learned a useful sentence,

386
00:17:16,260 --> 00:17:18,220
subscribe to The Cozy French

387
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:21,360
to keep practising with us.

388
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:24,380
Léa: You can listen again to our little conversation

389
00:17:24,380 --> 00:17:27,620
between neighbours and try both roles.

390
00:17:27,670 --> 00:17:29,960
Mathieu: Thank you for sharing this moment.

391
00:17:29,960 --> 00:17:31,250
Wherever you are,

392
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:34,920
we wish you a lovely rest of your day.

393
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:39,030
Léa: Take care of yourselves and see you very soon!

394
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:41,750
Are you going to sit down for a bit now?

395
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:44,480
Mathieu: Yes. My armchair is waiting for me.

396
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:49,280
I'm finally going to fill in the first box on my card.

397
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:51,450
Léa: Careful not to turn

398
00:17:51,450 --> 00:17:53,760
breaks into a race for points.

399
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:55,360
Mathieu: You're right.

400
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:57,100
I'm putting the card down.

401
00:17:57,150 --> 00:18:00,640
This time, I'm just sitting down, that's all.
